Pointer round-trips — `*&v` and `&*p`
======================================

The two unary operators `&` (address-of) and `*` (dereference)
are inverses on the value/address axis:

  - `*&v` recovers the value at `v`'s address; the result equals
    `v`. The pointer `&v` is computed and immediately discarded.
  - `&*p` is the pointer's own address (after a no-op load).
    Reading the pointer's contents then taking the address of
    that "lvalue" must yield `p` itself.

These tests pin both directions on a scalar, struct field,
and ARC ref:

  - The expression compiles as expected on every backend
    (the AST shape may produce overlap between an lvalue load
    and a struct address — easy to mis-elaborate).
  - The result equals the original value/address.

Companion to `ptr_deref_basic.lsysl` (basic `*p`) and
`ptr_compare.lsysl` (pointer equality).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct PrtBox
        v: int
        next: int

`*&v` on a scalar int — recovers the value
------------------------------------------

    #test
    test_deref_addrof_scalar() -> unit
        var x: int = 42
        val y = *&x
        assert(y == 42, s"expected 42, got $y")
        // Mutate x; y was captured by value, so y stays 42 and x is updated.
        x = 99
        assert(y == 42, s"y captured by value, not aliased; got $y")
        assert(x == 99, s"x mutated; got $x")

`*&v` round-trip with a struct field
------------------------------------

The field is taken by address-of, then deref'd. The result is
the field's value at the time of the expression.

    #test
    test_deref_addrof_field() -> unit
        var b = PrtBox(7, 100)
        val v = *&b.v
        val n = *&b.next
        assert(v == 7, s"field v: expected 7, got $v")
        assert(n == 100, s"field next: expected 100, got $n")

`&*p` round-trips — addr-of-deref equals the original pointer
-------------------------------------------------------------

`p` is a pointer; `*p` is the value at `p`'s target; `&*p` is
the address of that target — which is just `p` again.

    #test
    test_addrof_deref_scalar() -> unit
        var x: int = 5
        val p: *int = &x
        val q: *int = &*p
        assert(p == q, "&*p should equal p")

Mutation through `&*p` reaches the original
--------------------------------------------

`&*p` produces the original target's address; writing through
that lvalue is the same as writing through `p`.

    #test
    test_mutation_via_addrof_deref() -> unit
        var x: int = 0
        val p: *int = &x
        val q: *int = &*p
        *q = 77
        assert(x == 77, s"x should be 77 after write through q; got $x")
        assert(*p == 77, s"*p should also see 77; got ${*p}")

Round-trip in a chain — `*&*&v`
-------------------------------

Double composition: same result as `v`.

    #test
    test_double_roundtrip_scalar() -> unit
        var x: int = 11
        val y = *&*&x
        assert(y == 11, s"double round-trip: expected 11, got $y")

`*&` round-trip on a `&T` ref's field via the implicit lvalue
-------------------------------------------------------------

A ref-typed binding `r: &PrtBox` exposes `r.v` as a field
read. `*&r.v` is the same as `r.v` itself.

    #test
    test_roundtrip_through_ref_field() -> unit
        val r: &PrtBox = new PrtBox(13, 26)
        val v = *&r.v
        val n = *&r.next
        assert(v == 13, s"r.v via round-trip: got $v")
        assert(n == 26, s"r.next via round-trip: got $n")

Round-trip across a function parameter
--------------------------------------

A function takes `*int`, returns `*int` after a round-trip
through `&*p`. The caller's pointer is unchanged in value.

    prt_roundtrip_param(p: *int) -> *int = &*p

    #test
    test_roundtrip_through_fn() -> unit
        var x: int = 99
        val p: *int = &x
        val q = prt_roundtrip_param(p)
        assert(p == q, "fn round-trip preserves pointer identity")
        // Mutate through q — reaches the original x.
        *q = 42
        assert(x == 42, s"mutation via returned pointer: x = $x")
